 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Non-convexity:** The optimization landscape is non-convex due to the complex NeRF density field. The optimizer may get stuck in local minima. Initialization quality (e.g., using A*) is critical [S1].
-*   **Density Proxy Assumption:** The method assumes NeRF density is a strong proxy for collision probability. This may fail in regions where the NeRF is poorly trained or ambiguous [S1].
-*   **Computational Cost:** Evaluating the NeRF for every body point at every waypoint is computationally expensive. Performance depends heavily on the number of waypoints and body points [S1].
+*   **Nonconvexity:** The trajectory optimization is fundamentally nonconvex, requiring robust first-order methods like Adam [S1].
+*   **NeRF Density Proxy:** The assumption that light ray termination probability proxies mass particle termination may not hold for all materials or densities, though it correlates well with ground truth mesh intersections [S1].
+*   **Initialization Sensitivity:** Poor initialization (e.g., straight line through obstacles) may lead to local minima, though the dense waypoint optimization helps mitigate this compared to minimum-snap planners [S1].
 
